---
/**
 * website/src/components/ScreenshotSlider.astro — the console's screenshots (owner feedback
 * 2026-10-19: "Console: we need a screenshot slider at the top"; the owner's approved board
 * `website/design-ref/ConsoleV2.dc.html`, 2026-10-20; owner feedback 2026-10-21: "console page:
 * screenshots missing").
 *
 * **What it is: a framed scroll container with snap points, and dots that are anchors.** The
 * track is a horizontally scrolling box with `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory`, so a swipe, a
 * trackpad, a scrollbar drag and the keyboard's own arrow keys all move it and the browser
 * lands on a whole screenshot rather than halfway between two; each dot is an `<a href="#…">`
 * pointing at its slide, so a reader who would rather not scroll gets there in one press —
 * the browser scrolling the track itself, because a fragment's target brings the scroll
 * container it sits in along with it (a jump to a slide that is off the right-hand edge does
 * not scroll the page past the slider). **Every one of those four ways in needs no script**,
 * and that is still the whole of the slider's fallback: the pictures are `<img>` elements in
 * the page, the dots are links, and a reader who runs no JavaScript reaches all four screens.
 *
 * **The pictures are the data file's rows, and there are four of them.** The owner's feedback
 * of 2026-10-21 was that the page's screenshots were missing: the frame drew labelled
 * placeholder slides while `website/data/console-screens.yaml` carried no row, and the rows
 * are there now — the Live, Run, Spend and Installations screens, captured from the console
 * build and served from `public/screenshots/`. Nothing about the frame moved with them: the
 * same track, the same snap points, the same dots and the same caption line, with each row's
 * own picture, `alt` and caption in the slide the placeholder used to draw. **A row that has
 * not arrived is no longer drawn as a box**: the section renders the rows it is handed, and a
 * data file that carried none would render no slider, no heading and no frame at all rather
 * than a picture-shaped hole a reader cannot tell from a broken image.
 *
 * **The first picture is not lazy and the rest are.** The first slide is the one the frame
 * shows, so it is fetched with the page (`loading="eager"`); the three a reader has to scroll
 * or press a dot to reach are fetched when they come near the viewport (`loading="lazy"`), and
 * every one of them declares `decoding="async"` so decoding a 1920-wide WebP does not hold up
 * the frame around it. The `width` and `height` attributes are the file's own pixels, read out
 * of the data file, and they are what reserves each slide's box before its bytes land — the
 * `aspect-ratio` the placeholder used to state as a literal, and the reason the page does not
 * jump as the pictures arrive.
 *
 * **The board's arrows and the count are here now, as the script's half of the control** (the
 * owner's feedback of 2026-10-21: *"the slider should show how many screenshots there are (dots
 * or "1 / 4" counter), and I should be able to click to select them (right now only the keyboard
 * works: add clickable dots/thumbnails and previous/next buttons)"*). The section carries a
 * control bar — a Previous button, the counter, a Next button — and **the markup emits it
 * `hidden` and only while there is more than one slide**: a one-slide slider has nowhere to go,
 * so it carries no control at all (`website/test/console-screens.mjs` holds that), and a reader
 * with no script sees the four screenshots and the four dots rather than two buttons that do
 * nothing. `public/scripts/site.js` reveals the bar, keeps the counter and the dots' current
 * mark in step with the track's scroll position, and advances a slide every six seconds while
 * nobody is reading. The arrows are drawn with a border and a rotation rather than as an
 * `<svg>`, the way the dots are drawn, so the bar costs the page no drawing and no second
 * file; each one carries its name in an `aria-label`, because a shape has no text in it.
 *
 * **The dots are still anchors, and that is what makes them clickable.** A dot press is
 * intercepted by the script and made the same move a Next press makes — one slide at a time,
 * with the counter and the current dot following — and with no script the anchor's own jump is
 * what a press does.
 *
 * **Keyboard and screen reader.** The track is focusable (`tabindex="0"`) and named, which
 * is what makes a scroll container scrollable by arrow keys at all and what tells a screen
 * reader what the box is; the dots carry no text of their own — they are drawn as dots — so
 * each one carries an accessible name; every picture carries `alt` and every slide a
 * `<figcaption>`, both read out of the data file rather than written here. **The page reads
 * with JavaScript off**, which is the whole of the fallback this slider needs: the pictures
 * are `<img>` elements in the page, the first of them is the one the frame opens on, and no
 * control on the page is drawn, moved or shown by script.
 *
 * **The autoplay is six seconds, and it stops for anyone who is reading** (the owner's feedback
 * of 2026-10-21: *"auto-slide every few seconds (~6s; pause on hover/focus and when the tab is
 * hidden; off for prefers-reduced-motion)"*). It is the script's, not the stylesheet's: the
 * interval lives in `public/scripts/site.js`, which pauses it while the pointer is over the
 * slider, while the focus is inside it and while the tab is hidden, and never starts it at all
 * for a visitor whose system asks for less motion (`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, D105 —
 * read at load and watched afterwards, the way the menu watches its own query). **The move
 * itself is not animated**: the script lands on the slide it asked for, because the counter is
 * the track's scroll position and a move animated by the browser is a move during which the
 * two disagree. The component's own stylesheet declares no `animation` at all, and the only
 * motion it states is `scroll-behavior: smooth` on the track — the reader's own scrolling and
 * the dots' anchors — which `Base.astro`'s global reduced-motion rule neutralises.
 *
 * **The slides are data.** `website/data/console-screens.yaml` carries one row per screenshot —
 * the file's name under `public/screenshots/`, its `alt`, its caption and its own pixel
 * dimensions — and this component renders the rows it is handed and nothing else. The facts
 * come in as props rather than being read here, the way `Downloads.astro` takes
 * its own: `website/src/pages/console.astro` loads and validates the data file through
 * `src/content.ts`'s `loadConsoleScreens()`, and this component renders what it was handed.
 * No `src` is composed here either — the loader derives each one from the row's file name,
 * so no file under `website/` carries a URL (Global Constraint 9).
 *
 * **The current slide is the script's reading of the track's scroll position**, which is the
 * one thing a stylesheet cannot see: the dot the reader is on carries `aria-current="true"`
 * (and every other dot `"false"`, so no attribute has to be taken off anything), and the
 * counter reads `n / N`. Both follow the reader's own swipe as much as the script's own moves,
 * because both are read from where the track actually is. **None of it is needed to read the
 * page**: with no script the dots are links, the track scrolls, and the four screenshots are
 * all in the document — which is the site's rule (D183) and the reason the fallback is a
 * property of the markup rather than a promise about the script.
 */
import type { ConsoleScreen } from '../content.ts'

interface Props {
  /**
   * The slides, in the order the data file lists them. An empty list renders the section at
   * all: there is no picture to draw and no frame to draw it in.
   */
  screens: ConsoleScreen[]
}

const { screens } = Astro.props

// The section's own heading and the hook `website/test/console-screens.mjs` reads: the
// section is the slider, and "the slider is there" is a statement about this element.
const HEADING = 'Screenshots'
const SLOT = 'console-screens'

// Each slide's id: what its dot points at, and what an anchor from anywhere else can name.
const slideId = (index: number) => `console-screen-${index + 1}`
---

{
  screens.length > 0 && (
    <section class="screens" data-slot={SLOT} data-slider aria-labelledby="console-screens-heading">
      <h2 id="console-screens-heading">{HEADING}</h2>

      {/* The frame: the panel the board draws the slider in, and the box the track scrolls
          inside. It is here rather than in the track's own rules because it is the frame that
          holds the dots' row and the slide's caption together with the picture. */}
      <div class="screens-frame">
        {/* The track. `tabindex="0"` is not decoration: a scroll container that cannot be
            focused cannot be scrolled by the keyboard, and the slides off the right-hand edge
            would be reachable only with a pointer. The label says what the box is, because a
            focusable region is announced as an unnamed group without one. */}
        <ul
          class="screens-track"
          tabindex="0"
          aria-label={`${HEADING} of the console. Scroll sideways to see the rest.`}
        >
          {screens.map((screen, index) => (
            <li class="screen" id={slideId(index)}>
              <figure class="screen-figure">
                <img
                  class="screen-image"
                  src={screen.src}
                  alt={screen.alt}
                  width={screen.width}
                  height={screen.height}
                  loading={index === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
                  decoding="async"
                />
                <figcaption class="screen-caption">{screen.caption}</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>

      {/* **The bar the script drives, and only while there is somewhere to go.** Two arrows
          and the count, emitted `hidden`: with no script they are not drawn at all, and the
          dots below plus the track are the whole control. The arrows carry their names in
          `aria-label` because a chevron drawn by the stylesheet has no text, and the counter
          is an empty live region — the script writes `n / N` into it, so a reader who has
          never seen the page before this build is not shown a number nothing keeps in step. */}
      {screens.length > 1 && (
        <div class="screens-bar" data-slider-bar hidden>
          <button type="button" class="screen-arrow" data-slider-prev aria-label="Previous screenshot" />
          <p class="screens-counter" data-slider-counter role="status" aria-live="polite" />
          <button type="button" class="screen-arrow" data-slider-next aria-label="Next screenshot" />
        </div>
      )}

      {/* One dot per slide, and none at all for a single slide: a control that can only
          take a reader where they already are is noise. Each dot is an anchor into its
          slide, so the whole control is the browser's own — and it carries its name in an
          `aria-label`, because a dot drawn by the stylesheet has no text for a screen
          reader to announce. */}
      {screens.length > 1 && (
        <ul class="screens-dots">
          {screens.map((_, index) => (
            <li class="screen-dot-item">
              <a
                class="screen-dot"
                href={`#${slideId(index)}`}
                aria-label={`Show screenshot ${index + 1} of ${screens.length}`}
              />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </section>
  )
}

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): every colour below is the palette in force, so the contrast check
     in `website/test/a11y.mjs` is about the colours this slider actually paints. */
  .screens {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xl);
  }

  /* The frame: the panel the board draws around the slide, and the room the slide and its
     caption are given inside it. The border is a hairline of the palette's own tones rather
     than a gradient, because the panel already sits a step above the ground and a second
     drawing on top of it is a shape the page does not need. */
  .screens-frame {
    padding: var(--space-sm);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-xl);
    background: var(--panel);
  }

  /* **The track is the scroller.** `grid-auto-flow: column` with a full-width column puts
     one slide per "page" of the box, and `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory` makes the browser
     land on one of them: a swipe that ends halfway through a screenshot settles on the
     nearest edge, so a reader never sits between two pictures. `overscroll-behavior-x`
     keeps a swipe at the end of the track from turning into the browser's back gesture on
     the phones that do that. */
  .screens-track {
    display: grid;
    grid-auto-flow: column;
    grid-auto-columns: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  }

  .screen {
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }

  /* The figure is the slide: its picture and the line under it, and no margin of its own —
     the track's padding is the only space around it. */
  .screen-figure {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The picture takes the slide's width and its own aspect ratio, which the `width` and
     `height` attributes reserve before the file lands (base.css caps an image at its
     container's width and keeps its ratio). The border is what separates a screenshot from
     the page it sits on, including the ones whose own background is the page's colour. */
  .screen-image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .screen-caption {
    margin: var(--space-2xs) 0 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  }

  /* The bar: the two arrows and the count, centred under the frame and above the dots. **No
     `display` here**, deliberately: the markup emits it `hidden`, and a `display` declaration
     of our own would win over the user agent's `[hidden]` rule and put two buttons that do
     nothing on a page whose script never arrived (the same note `base.css` carries over the
     copy control). Its children are inline, so the row is centred by `text-align` and spaced
     by their own margins rather than by a `gap`. */
  .screens-bar {
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* An arrow: 44 px of box with a chevron drawn in its middle — a border and a rotation, the
     way the dots are drawn, so the bar adds no drawing to the page. The one control here that
     is a `<button>` rather than a link, so it carries the focus ring `base.css` gives every
     `:focus-visible` element and the pointer a reader expects of a control. */
  .screen-arrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 44px;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0;
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--text);
    cursor: pointer;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  .screen-arrow::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    border: solid currentColor;
    border-width: 0 2px 2px 0;
    transform: rotate(135deg);
  }

  /* The two chevrons are one drawing: the next arrow is the previous one's shape the other
     way round, so the two end up pointing opposite ways without a second border rule. */
  .screen-arrow[data-slider-next]::before {
    transform: rotate(-45deg);
  }

  .screen-arrow:hover {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--accent-on-dark);
  }

  .screen-arrow:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* The count: `n / N`, written by the script. Tabular figures, so the line does not jog
     sideways as the number of digits changes, at the size of a caption rather than of prose. */
  .screens-counter {
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 5ch;
    margin: 0 var(--space-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  /* The dots: one per slide, in a row under the frame. They are the second way to a slide
     and the only control here that is a link, so they take the focus ring `base.css` gives
     every `:focus-visible` element. */
  .screens-dots {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* A dot is a target a reader taps, so it is at least 44 px of box with the dot drawn
     inside it: the board draws a 10 px dot, and a 10 px target is not one a finger can hit
     (the owner's brief of 2026-10-19 measures every control against 44 px). It is written as
     a minimum rather than a height, because that is the declaration the site's one
     reading of the brief looks for — `website/test/lib/controls.mjs`, which the home
     page's case and this page's both call — and because the box may grow: the dot is centred
     in whatever the anchor's content comes to. */
  .screen-dot-item {
    display: flex;
  }

  .screen-dot {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 44px;
    min-height: 44px;
  }

  /* The dot itself, drawn by the anchor's own pseudo-element so that the target above is
     invisible and the mark is the small pill the board draws. */
  .screen-dot::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: var(--space-xs);
    height: var(--space-xs);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .screen-dot:hover::before {
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* The slide the reader is on: the script writes `aria-current="true"` on its own dot, and
     the mark is filled for it — the same fill `:focus-visible` draws, so the dot a reader has
     just pressed and the dot they are looking at are told apart by the ring rather than by the
     mark. A stylesheet cannot read a scroll position, which is why the attribute is the
     script's; what the attribute *paints* is this rule's. */
  .screen-dot[aria-current='true']::before {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  .screen-dot:focus-visible::before {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
